---
import ChartScale from './ChartScale.astro';
import FinancialViews from './FinancialViews.astro';
import ForecastChartControls from './ForecastChartControls.astro';
---
<section id="forecast-view" class="view" hidden>
  <header class="page-heading"><div><p class="eyebrow">02 / CASH-FLOW PROJECTIONS</p><h1>A little foresight.</h1><p class="subtitle">Explore up to five years of cash flow. Compare methods and see how your assumptions change the path.</p></div><ChartScale id="forecast-scale" /></header>
  <FinancialViews />
  <div class="forecast-layout">
    <article class="card assumptions"><div class="card-heading"><div><p class="eyebrow">START HERE</p><h2>Your starting point</h2></div></div>
    <label>Starting balances<select id="balance-source"><option value="manual">Enter a combined starting point</option><option value="accounts">Use matching account balance dates</option><option value="latest">Use latest values as estimates</option></select></label>
    <p id="balance-source-note" class="input-note">Enter combined balances or choose your dated account balances.</p>
    <label>Available cash <span>NOK</span><input id="cash" type="number" step="0.01" placeholder="Enter current balance"></label>
      <p class="input-note">Cash you can spend, excluding the savings reserve below.</p>
      <label>Savings reserve <span>NOK</span><input id="savings" type="number" min="0" step="0.01" placeholder="0"></label>
      <label>Minimum cash buffer <span>NOK</span><input id="buffer" type="number" min="0" step="0.01" placeholder="0"></label>
      <label>Balance date<input id="balance-date" type="date"></label>
      <div class="form-divider"></div><h3>Monthly assumptions</h3>
      <label>Use history from<select id="lookback"><option value="1">Last complete month</option><option value="3" selected>Last 3 complete months</option><option value="6">Last 6 complete months</option><option value="12">Last 12 complete months</option></select></label>
      <p id="baseline-caption" class="input-note"></p>
      <label>Regular income <span>NOK / month</span><input id="income" type="number" min="0" step="0.01"></label>
      <label class="checkbox"><input id="income-growth" type="checkbox" aria-describedby="income-trend-note" /> Apply income trend to all projections</label>
      <p id="income-trend-note" class="input-note" aria-live="polite"></p>
      <label><span id="expense-label">Spending & bills</span> <span>NOK / month</span><input id="expense" type="number" min="0" step="0.01"></label>
      <label>Savings & investments <span>NOK / month</span><input id="saving" type="number" min="0" step="0.01"></label>
      <p class="input-note">Contributions reduce available cash. The views above also track their destination in reserves or investments. Optional growth assumptions apply to the savings and investment paths.</p>
      <p id="forecast-spending-plans-note" class="input-note" aria-live="polite" hidden></p>
      <div id="plan-expense-review" hidden><p id="plan-expense-review-copy" class="input-note" role="status"></p><button id="reset-plan-expense" type="button" class="text-button">Use spending from history</button><button id="confirm-plan-expense" type="button" class="text-button">Confirm this spending amount</button></div>
      <button id="manage-spending-plans" type="button" class="text-button">Manage spending pots →</button>
      <button id="reset-baseline" class="text-button">↺ Use averages from history</button>
      <button id="large-payments" class="text-button">Review large historical payments →</button>
    </article>
    <div class="forecast-results">
      <article class="card"><div class="card-heading"><div><p class="eyebrow">YOUR POSSIBLE PATH</p><h2>Funds over time</h2></div><div class="forecast-time-controls"><label class="forecast-horizon">History shown<select id="forecast-history"><option value="0">Future only</option><option value="1">1 month</option><option value="3" selected>3 months</option><option value="6">6 months</option><option value="12">12 months</option><option value="24">24 months</option></select></label><label class="forecast-horizon">Forecast horizon<select id="forecast-horizon">{Array.from({ length: 60 }, (_, i) => i + 1).map(months => <option value={months} selected={months === 3}>{months} {months === 1 ? 'month' : 'months'}{months % 12 === 0 ? ` · ${months / 12} ${months === 12 ? 'year' : 'years'}` : ''}</option>)}</select></label></div></div>
        <ForecastChartControls />
        <div class="scenario-row"><span class="muted small">Try a scenario</span><button data-scenario="same" class="scenario selected">Current pace</button><button data-scenario="less" class="scenario">Spend 10% less</button><button data-scenario="more" class="scenario">Spend 10% more</button></div>
        <p id="forecast-history-note" class="input-note"></p>
        <div id="forecast-chart" class="forecast-chart"></div>
        <p id="forecast-chart-history-note" class="input-note"></p>
        <p class="input-note">Longer horizons amplify differences between assumptions. These lines are alternative projections, not confidence bounds or probabilities.</p>
        <dl id="forecast-stats" class="statistics-grid statistics-grid-three forecast-stat-cards"></dl>
        <div class="scenario-inputs"><label>Monthly spending change <span>NOK / month</span><input id="adjustment" type="number" step="0.01" value="0"><small>Negative means spending less.</small></label><label>One-off expense in month 1 <span>NOK</span><input id="one-off" type="number" min="0" step="0.01" value="0"></label></div>
        <div id="forecast-message" class="forecast-message" aria-live="polite"></div>
      </article>
      <article class="card"><div class="card-heading"><h2>Compare cash outcomes</h2><button id="open-forecast-methods" type="button" class="outline-button forecast-method-trigger" aria-haspopup="dialog" aria-controls="forecast-method-dialog">Cash table methods <span id="forecast-method-count" class="muted small"></span></button></div><div id="forecast-comparison"></div></article>
      <article class="card"><div class="card-heading"><h2>Month by month</h2><span class="muted small">Rolling monthly estimates</span></div><div id="forecast-table"></div></article>
      <article class="card" aria-labelledby="scenario-insights-title"><div class="card-heading"><div><p class="eyebrow">PUT THE SCENARIO IN CONTEXT</p><h2 id="scenario-insights-title">What shapes your forecast</h2></div></div><dl id="scenario-insights" class="statistics-grid statistics-grid-three"></dl></article>
      <details class="method-note"><summary>How this forecast works</summary><p>The baseline uses the average of complete months before the earlier of the balance month and latest transaction month. Regular income uses rows marked as salary or “Inntekt”. Other incoming money, refunds, and interest do not become recurring income automatically. Forecast cash flows use the available-cash account boundary; transactions whose signed account is unknown retain their existing classification.</p><p>Cash = starting cash + regular income − spending − savings contributions − spending change. The one-off expense is subtracted in month 1. The cash comparison shows balances before reserve support. Cash &amp; reserves models optional withdrawals and credits planned reserve contributions. Wealth credits allocated contributions once and applies optional savings and investment growth to opening values before each month’s contribution. Unallocated contributions leave the tracked total. Internal transfers are excluded from spending. Mark one-off historical transactions in Transactions to leave them out of the baseline.</p><p>The income-trend toggle applies to every projection, including your scenario and unchanged assumptions. With it off, each method uses its original income estimates. With it on, each method’s income is multiplied by the same historical growth factors; its spending and savings estimates stay intact. Zero income stays zero. The trend uses up to 36 complete months, requires 24 months of coverage and 18 positive income months, and compares matching calendar months across years. A latest-three-month median anchors the learned growth factors, with 0.98 damping each month. Declines are supported. Your scenario keeps your entered income as its base; the monthly input is not overwritten. Monthly summary cards average projected income across the selected horizon. Manual inputs remain available for known pay changes or reimbursements. Historical average, last month, and exponential smoothing estimate each of income, spending and savings separately from the selected complete months, then hold those base estimates constant before applying any enabled income trend. Smoothing starts from the first complete month and rounds each update to integer øre. Seasonal repeat uses the last 12 complete months and repeats them by calendar month, even for horizons beyond a year. History ends before the earlier of the balance month and latest transaction month. Months without recorded activity inside that history count as zero; imports may be incomplete. Manual monthly inputs affect your scenario and unchanged assumptions only. Spending changes and the month-one expense apply to your scenario and all history methods; unchanged assumptions omits both. This estimate does not model payday, bill dates, tax changes, or daily cash lows. Savings and investment growth is included only through the explicit growth controls above. End-of-month estimates can look healthy even if a bill is due before payday. It is a scenario calculation, not a bank balance or a guarantee. Coincident lines mean methods produced the same balances; inspect the chart figures or comparison tables for each method.</p><p>Square history markers show recorded totals only when every included account has a known observation on the same date. Missing observations leave gaps. Dotted cash history is reconstructed backwards from your starting cash using recorded cash movements. It includes historical one-offs and all incoming money, regardless of forecast exclusions, and is an estimate rather than an observed balance. Missing payments or a gap between the latest transaction and balance date can make this reconstruction incomplete. The History shown control changes the chart only; Use history from controls the estimation window.</p><p>Spending pots with a monthly pace replace their category in historical estimates and add their planned purchases once to every method. Pot spending is capped by its recorded balance plus planned internal funding. Funding moves existing available cash between accounts and never adds income. A blank pace tracks the pot without replacing history; individual one-off exclusions still apply. Pot dates must match the forecast date when a pace is set. The monthly cash-change card averages planned pot spending over the chosen horizon. Monthly spending-change scenarios leave pot schedules unchanged.</p><p>Method background: <a href="https://otexts.com/fpp3/simple-methods.html" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">simple forecasting methods</a> and <a href="https://otexts.com/fpp3/ses.html" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">exponential smoothing</a> and <a href="https://otexts.com/fpp3/holt.html" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">damped trends</a> in Forecasting: Principles and Practice. Asset growth uses the <a href="https://www.gipsstandards.org/standards/gips-standards-for-firms/gips-standards-handbook-for-firms/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Modified Dietz cash-flow adjustment</a>, an approximation that needs complete contributions and withdrawals. At least a year of common dated observations is required before applying an annualized historical rate; large flows and volatile prices can reduce its accuracy.</p></details>
    </div>
  </div>
  <dialog id="forecast-method-dialog" aria-labelledby="forecast-method-dialog-title" aria-describedby="forecast-method-dialog-note">
    <form method="dialog">
      <div class="dialog-heading"><h2 id="forecast-method-dialog-title">Cash comparison methods</h2><button type="submit" class="icon-button" aria-label="Close projection methods">×</button></div>
      <p id="forecast-method-dialog-note" class="input-note">Select methods for the cash comparison tables. Chart layers controls the methods and fund combinations drawn in the chart. Changes are saved as you select.</p>
      <fieldset class="forecast-methods"><legend>Compare projection methods</legend><div id="forecast-method-options"></div></fieldset>
      <div class="dialog-actions"><button type="submit" class="primary-button">Done</button></div>
    </form>
  </dialog>
</section>
